<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stabat Mater</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stabat Mater is a 13th-century Christian hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. It portrays her suffering as a mother at the crucifixion of her son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Its title comes from its first line. The hymn has long been ascribed to the Franciscan friar Jacopone da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todi">Todi</a> (c. 1230–1306), and also to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Stabat Mater</b> is a 13th-century Christian hymn to the Virgin Mary.</blockquote><a href="/songs/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a></li><li><blockquote>A fifth, the &quot;<b>Stabat Mater</b>&quot;, was added by Pope Benedict XIII in 1727.</blockquote><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes</a></li></ul></section>
